00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.870
Today my friend Adam is
challenging me to make macarons

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.400
shaped as corgis.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.050
It's this shop called Honey &amp;
Butter in Irvine, California

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.010
that makes a bunch of
different types of macarons,

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.350
one of them is this
thing called a uni-corgi.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.830
A uni-corgi is a corgi
with a unicorn horn.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.450
It looks really easy
based off of the video

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.140
'cause it shows her just
like piping the shape

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.890
of a corgi and then they
fill it with what looks like

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.760
a chocolatey filling and they kind of just

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.760
pop it right on top.

00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:32.783
Everyone loves a macaron.

00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:36.250
Never made macarons and I've
never made any type of food

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.840
in the shape of an animal before.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.550
As always today, very talented,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.840
Tasty chef producer Rie is here.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.110
How hard is it really?

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:43.943
- In my opinion,

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.020
it's one of the most
difficult design to make.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.540
So, you just need to practice.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.740
- The feet of a macaron
is when the cookie rises

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.240
and so that's like an
important mark of a macaron.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.660
So, if you don't have
feet, that's a bad macaron.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:56.493
Great.

00:00:56.864 --> 00:00:57.697
If I struggle, I'll give you a call.

00:00:57.697 --> 00:00:59.040
- Okay.
- I found a Tasty recipe,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.900
I'm gonna just make regular
circular macarons first

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.240
and then if that goes well,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.630
I will graduate to the uni-corgi.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:07.760
Look out world.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.100
What if I'm so good at this?

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.440
So, I printed out a template,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.610
I've seen other people use
it when they pipe it out

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.110
so that it's relatively
the same size and shape.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.233
So, ,that's underneath
a piece of baking paper.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.640
200 grams of powdered sugar,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.633
110 grams of almond flour,

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.260
100 grams of egg whites.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:25.093
Shabam.

00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:26.600
Stripe city, population me.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.213
50 grams of sugar for my meringue.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.393
Big bowl.

00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:32.969
An important thing is
that the almond flour

00:01:33.379 --> 00:01:35.160
and the powdered sugar
are as fine as possible.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.830
So, the way people do
that is to throw it in

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.033
the food processor.

00:01:40.448 --> 00:01:41.912
(laughing)

00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:43.022
What am I doing wrong?

00:01:43.022 --> 00:01:44.019
There we go.

00:01:44.019 --> 00:01:47.190
Oh yeah, this (bleeping) gonna be fine.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.530
So, I'm gonna make my meringue

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.693
which is like the base for this cookie.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:51.953
A little splootsch of tartar.

00:01:51.953 --> 00:01:53.540
And then I'm just gonna whip 'em.

00:01:54.121 --> 00:01:56.740
That will give us the
firmness and structure

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.623
for this light and fluffy cookie.

00:01:59.001 --> 00:01:59.970
What a weird thing.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.793
These things come out of chickens.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.263
Oh, that looks pretty stiff.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.643
Oh, what's that thing?

00:02:04.758 --> 00:02:06.980
I'm really scared to do this.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.980
- What are you doing?
- If you can like

00:02:09.182 --> 00:02:10.210
